  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nWith the return of onshore flow, moisture is expected to continue \npooling ahead of our next cold front. That front is expected to \nsweep through the region this morning, with a reinforcing surge of \nhigh pressure behind it. This will allow for cooler temperatures to \nremain balmy and mild through the end of next week. Some medium to \nhigh rain chances (40-80%) will exist along the front as it moves \nthrough South Texas today. Best chances will likely be in the \nafternoon period. An isolated risk for strong to severe storms will \nbe expected today. SPC has maintained the Coastal Bend in a Marginal \nRisk (1 out of 5) for severe weather with the primary risk being \nlarge hail. Low-level moisture convergence and favorable forcing \nalong the cold front will act as the primary focus for convective \ninitiation. Combined with a moderate to very unstable air mass (CAPE \nof 1,200-2000 J/kg;Lifted indices of -4 to -7) and 0-6 km bulk shear \naround 45-50 kts supports strong to severe storm development. The \ncaveat will be the speed of the front which could limit the time \nthose storms have to develop in the environment. Will continue to \nmonitor this over the next few model runs. \n\nA secondary front will push through on Tuesday, ensuring a chilly \nThanksgiving across the area. High temperatures will be in the upper \n50s to low 60s! Lows on Tuesday night and Wednesday night will be \nthe coldest and there is a possibility that we could see \ntemperatures flirt near freezing. Will be something else to keep in \nmind as we progress through this week.\n\nMinor coastal flooding will remain a threat through the remainder of \nthe weekend as higher than normal tides and long period swells \ncontinue. We will likely see these conditions continue through the \nearly part of this week and another extension maybe needed.",
